# Line endings, declared rather than left to each developer's core.autocrlf.
#
# This matters more here than in most repositories, because the file editor
# tool preserves whatever line endings a file already has (see the note in
# core/claude_learned_schemas.py). Without a policy, what it preserves depends
# on how the person who cloned the repo had git configured, and two developers
# would produce whole-file diffs at each other.
#
# Text is stored and checked out as LF. Nothing on modern Windows requires
# CRLF for these file types — Python, PowerShell, VS Code and Notepad all read
# LF — and pinning it means the working tree matches the repository regardless
# of git configuration.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exceptions, and they are real ones. cmd.exe parses a batch file line by
# line as it executes it, and mis-handles some constructs when the terminator
# is a bare LF; PowerShell is more tolerant but conventionally CRLF, and a
# signed script's signature block is whitespace-sensitive.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf

# Never touched, in case any land here later.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pyd binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
